The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
doi_def->map.std->lvl.local_size = nla_get_u32(nla_b) + 1;
doi_def->map.std->lvl.local = kcalloc(doi_def->map.std->lvl.local_size, sizeof(u32), GFP_KERNEL);
doi_def->map.std->lvl.cipso = kcalloc(doi_def->map.std->lvl.cipso_size, sizeof(u32), GFP_KERNEL);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because excessively large values for `doi_def->map.std->lvl.local_size` and `doi_def->map.std->lvl.cipso_size` are derived from user input, which can lead to warnings when these values are passed to `kcalloc()` for memory allocation, especially if they exceed the limits of the system.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
doi_def->map.std->lvl.local = kcalloc(doi_def->map.std->lvl.local_size, sizeof(u32), GFP_KERNEL | __GFP_NOWARN);
doi_def->map.std->lvl.cipso = kcalloc(doi_def->map.std->lvl.cipso_size, sizeof(u32), GFP_KERNEL | __GFP_NOWARN);
```
Reason:
The patch fixes the bug by adding the `__GFP_NOWARN` flag to the `kcalloc()` calls, which suppresses warnings related to allocation size exceeding limits. This acknowledges that the values originate from user space and may not always be valid, thus preventing unnecessary warnings while still allowing the allocation to proceed.

